00:12:37,152 --> 00:12:42,436
So we have three features that make it smart than pure random fuzzing.

97
00:12:42,752 --> 00:12:46,512
The first feature is we call it motive gene.

98
00:12:46,592 --> 00:12:51,268
We introduced a high-level type of event to mimic human behaviors.

99
00:12:51,950 --> 00:12:55,172
Taking example here, this is uh retribution screen.

100
00:12:55,598 --> 00:13:02,112
For today's agent, it'll be very trivial for agent to fill the form and proceed to the next screen for coverage.

101
00:13:02,286 --> 00:13:05,872
However, for the machines 10 years ago, it's a hard problem.

102
00:13:06,158 --> 00:13:13,572
So we introduced the type of high-level sequence called MultifGene to simulate human behaviors to improve coverage.

103
00:13:15,470 --> 00:13:20,676
And second feature is S-Pings perform genetic search to design test cases.

104
00:13:21,262 --> 00:13:30,170
It starts by initializing multiple test sequences, and each sequence is composed of a list of system-level events.

105
00:13:30,752 --> 00:13:37,290
for example, here we have let's see, the T ones are click events and the pink ones are type events.

106
00:13:37,998 --> 00:13:42,660
And then it applies a bunch of genetic operators to improve the test.

107
00:13:43,118 --> 00:13:48,708
The selection would select parent two sequences to breed the next offspring.

108
00:13:50,126 --> 00:14:06,500
And it may select test cases with good properties like high coverage or good strong fault like uh fault revolution, and then crossover would swap some of the events for diversity, and the mutation would mutate from one event to another.

109
00:14:07,726 --> 00:14:16,100
This will be iterated many times for converging into the objectives with better properties.

110
00:14:18,478 --> 00:14:22,692
The third feature is Sapings is a multi-objective optimization approach.

111
00:14:23,694 --> 00:14:28,112
It might be trivial to just optimize one objective, let's say coverage.

112
00:14:28,430 --> 00:14:32,900
To improve that, you may want to throw many as many events as possible into the system.

113
00:14:33,358 --> 00:14:40,432
However, just like random fuzing, this would generate very long and uh uh sequences that are hard to debug or repro.

114
00:14:41,614 --> 00:14:50,436
So instead, C Pings takes a multi-objective optimization approach to maximize fault revolution, coverage, and meanwhile minimizing test sequence length.

115
00:14:50,992 --> 00:15:03,588
It tries to find a set of test cases that are optimal in terms of you cannot gain on one property without compromising on the other, and this is known as the protofront.

116
00:15:06,126 --> 00:15:08,752
So, why short sequences is so important?

117
00:15:09,390 --> 00:15:16,516
So, in practice, we believe uh crash only matters if it's actionable and developer could repro, debug, and fix.

