00:24:52,400 --> 00:25:02,852
And for its front crashes, we feed that into an automated fix system called SAPFix, which would generate valid patches for a developer to review.

197
00:25:04,302 --> 00:25:07,908
Now, after 10 years, did we get everything right?

198
00:25:08,814 --> 00:25:10,240
And the answer is not quite.

199
00:26:23,534 --> 00:26:32,580
Although in practice we gradually shifted to more practical objectives, like time cost, false poster rate, and developer experience.

200
00:26:35,470 --> 00:26:42,596
Limitations of the paper, uh first one, obviously we only tackled one type of bug, which is crash.

201
00:26:43,470 --> 00:26:50,880
And in practice, we need more types of bug to be covered, like crackness and other reliability type of bugs.

202
00:26:51,120 --> 00:26:56,772
And second, we measured coverage and crash count, not whether anyone acted.

203
00:26:57,134 --> 00:27:08,036
So actually, in the original SciP's paper, at that time, I think the state of art was to evaluate against open source apps and compare the reviewed crashes.

204
00:27:08,558 --> 00:27:20,868
I think we in the original paper went a bit ahead, actually, also tested on closed source apps, and we tried to contact developers to confirm the true positive, which already went a bit further, but I think it's still a bit shallow.

205
00:27:21,934 --> 00:27:25,120
The third limitation is we test it on released app.

206
00:27:25,440 --> 00:27:28,640
We don't need source code, which is a benefit and also limitation.

207
00:27:28,800 --> 00:27:31,280
Without source code, we cannot do bug prevention.

208
00:27:31,360 --> 00:27:34,788
We can only capture it when the bug was introduced to the system.

209
00:27:38,606 --> 00:27:45,316
There are three things we there are no way we could discuss in the original paper and we learned later.

210
00:27:45,742 --> 00:27:46,948
First one, the speed.

211
00:27:48,078 --> 00:27:57,284
We didn't expect we just only took three months to find a company, and later about one and a half year to production at the industry environment.

212
00:27:58,766 --> 00:28:00,772
And the second, portability.

213
00:28:01,710 --> 00:28:11,364
The urgent SPN's idea and paper focused on the crash finding with the evolution of search, and we designed a search engine for generating test cases.

214
00:28:12,014 --> 00:28:19,108
However, that engine was also used for privacy analysis later and also worked surprisingly well.

215
00:28:20,238 --> 00:28:25,380
Third, there was no major large language models at the time, and there was no agent.

216
00:28:26,000 --> 00:28:33,860
So we assumed the code generation and test generation was the expensive part, but nowadays it becomes the cheapest part.

217
00:28:34,000 --> 00:28:39,140
And validation and test and verification itself becomes heavy and expensive.

218
00:28:41,966 --> 00:28:44,896
So, part three, things start to shift left.

219
00:28:45,056 --> 00:28:50,724
By shift left, we mean to move the testing verification to an earlier stage of the development lifecycle.

220
00:28:51,630 --> 00:28:54,980
In the original SPN's paper, we deployed at post-release time.

221
00:28:55,456 --> 00:29:01,536
And later, when we went to Facebook, we moved that to continuous integration at post-land time.

222
00:29:01,616 --> 00:29:05,508
It's a bit earlier for continuous testing and verification.

223
00:29:06,222 --> 00:29:12,708
And then we further move that left to code review time so that we can prevent the bug in the first place.

224
00:29:14,798 --> 00:29:17,220
So you may ask why we want to shift left.

225
00:29:17,776 --> 00:29:19,456
And the answer is obvious.

226
00:29:19,616 --> 00:29:29,380
We shift left by shifting the test verification left, we'll find bug at a much earlier stage when the developer or today's agent still have the local contacts.

227
00:29:29,696 --> 00:29:31,268
It's much easier to fix.

228
00:29:31,856 --> 00:29:41,252
And if the bug slips through to later stage, it's really harder to root course, and there's a lower chance to be fixed for that bug.

229
00:29:44,750 --> 00:29:56,420
So one dimension is to shift left by moving the test modification earlier, and the other dimension is to extending to more type of bugs found.

230
00:29:56,878 --> 00:29:59,780
So here, let's take privacy as an example.

231
00:30:00,398 --> 00:30:05,348
We introduced a system called PrivacyCat at Easter 2024 ZipTrack.

232
00:30:06,096 --> 00:30:24,256
And this system reused the SIPIN's evolution search engine to drive the app behavior, and then the privacy cat system would monitor the app against a bunch of privacy policies to check the data flows and check whether if any data flows to the places that they shouldn't.

233
00:30:24,942 --> 00:30:31,776
So it used exactly the existing SPINS loop, explore, observe, judge, and report.

234
00:30:31,936 --> 00:30:35,396
And that judge is about checking the data flow properties.

235
00:30:36,398 --> 00:30:50,110
Since its deployment, PrefaceCat was able to prevent 74% of the in-scope privacy incident at the early stage, and it found over 493 potential privacy incidents.

236
00:30:51,886 --> 00:30:55,684
Here shows the high-level architecture on how this looks like.

237
00:30:56,302 --> 00:31:01,508
You can see that SIPINGS is in the lower bottom, the left side.

238
00:32:00,526 --> 00:32:04,452
However, both articles cannot answer questions like this.

239
00:32:07,022 --> 00:32:09,508
Does this change to what the author intended?

240
00:32:09,998 --> 00:32:12,900
Does it break an environment that nobody wrote down?

241
00:32:13,422 --> 00:32:16,548
And is this a right fix or just make the test pass?

242
00:32:18,670 --> 00:32:22,496
So in practice, this is really important problem for a real system.

243
00:32:22,990 --> 00:32:28,708
There are likely no crash, there is no rule it's broken, but it could behave incorrectly.

244
00:32:29,198 --> 00:32:32,900
And for those cases, we don't have our code to check.

245
00:32:33,518 --> 00:32:39,044
Today we could leverage large length models, but also it's not reliable.

246
00:32:39,216 --> 00:32:42,500
So we could talk about that in the part four of this talk.

247
00:32:47,744 --> 00:32:51,344
So let's put the past decade on the map again.

248
00:32:51,504 --> 00:32:59,584
We can see that we shifted left from post land to differ view time for crash finding, but the judge rarely changed.

249
00:32:59,664 --> 00:33:02,596
We are still finding reliability and crash issues.

250
00:33:02,926 --> 00:33:11,172
For correctness, we are still relying on developer written test and the developer authored policies like privacy policies to detect violations.

251
00:33:12,462 --> 00:33:20,932
To make the testing verification work at each of the stages, we need a reliable R code that could help check the correctness.

252
00:33:21,902 --> 00:33:28,164
And that's the gap we have today, and that's why I mentioned the deployment would follow R code.

253
00:33:28,384 --> 00:33:31,268
And for now, we don't have a reliable solution.

254
00:33:32,590 --> 00:33:33,424
Now, part four.

255
00:33:33,518 --> 00:33:36,420
To mitigate that gap, let's talk about the agent.

256
00:33:37,774 --> 00:33:40,676
In the agentic era, things are shifting.

257
00:33:41,806 --> 00:33:43,984
Generation is getting really cheap.

258
00:33:44,110 --> 00:33:45,796
However, verification did not.

259
00:33:47,790 --> 00:33:51,236
Today, agent can generate code changes faster than ever.

260
00:33:51,694 --> 00:33:57,924
However, the workload is mostly on human side, and human need to review those code to ensure quality.

261
00:33:58,478 --> 00:34:03,264
And the human attention does not scale with the development of the model.

262
00:34:03,758 --> 00:34:07,268
So that creates a gap as shown here in the chart.

263
00:34:07,982 --> 00:34:12,676
The human recapacity is not catching up with the volume of code generated.

264
00:34:13,390 --> 00:34:20,868
So this is, I think this is now one of the most significant problem in the new South Energy paradigm.

265
00:34:21,646 --> 00:34:23,556
And it's a testing verification problem.

266
00:34:24,494 --> 00:34:33,220
We need R code that agent can use to check the correctness and review itself during the different life stage of this development lifecycle.

267
00:34:35,214 --> 00:34:43,428
One reaction to the problem is might be just maybe we should just generate less to match the capacity that a human can review, right?

268
00:34:44,624 --> 00:34:53,348
And however, I think in practice, sometimes we should slow down a bit, but in practice, with AI, the whole industry is accelerating nowadays.

269
00:34:54,606 --> 00:34:58,148
So in my opinion, it's better to cap the risk, not the volume.

270
00:35:00,334 --> 00:35:06,464
Human attention will quickly get used up with the volume of the agent-generated code.

271
00:35:06,624 --> 00:35:14,564
However, if we document the human knowledge and write author some rules, and those rules could be reused to check every future change.

272
00:35:14,926 --> 00:35:17,124
So that could be part of the mitigation.

273
00:35:18,542 --> 00:35:21,444
And with agent, now we have the third type of R code.

274
00:35:22,318 --> 00:35:27,140
We call the learned R code, which can be used to check correctness behaviors.

275
00:35:28,078 --> 00:35:32,388
And this R code can be used to check correctness at least in two ways.

276
00:35:33,070 --> 00:35:37,924
First, statically, we can deploy an agent reviewer to review the code changes.

277
00:35:39,024 --> 00:35:57,060
And second, the agent now can run app dynamically, end-to-end, and infer the agent and check against the app screenshot and logs to check if any state like GUI state issues or expected state mismatch.

278
00:35:58,254 --> 00:36:01,424
And this is kind of knowledge we used to rely on human.

279
00:36:01,504 --> 00:36:03,300
Now we can leverage the agent.

280
00:36:04,078 --> 00:36:06,692
But the problem is the agent sometimes could be wrong.

281
00:36:07,566 --> 00:36:11,396
If we always listen to the agent, it's likely worse than no oracle at all.

282
00:36:14,446 --> 00:36:18,852
So now the question is how can we use Oracle that sometimes is wrong?

283
00:36:19,918 --> 00:36:22,820
Part of that answer is we pair it with policies.

284
00:36:23,470 --> 00:36:30,084
The agent would report its findings and we pair with human policies to decide the severity of the reported issues.

285
00:36:30,606 --> 00:36:41,860
For example, a suggestion from the agent is it's like if a suggestion is wrong, it's easy to cost a little from the human side with little extra attention.

286
00:36:42,440 --> 00:36:52,420
Well, if the agent reported an issue that's wrongly blocked the diff changes, that cost trust, and developers may start ignore every future changes.

287
00:36:53,134 --> 00:37:03,364
So in practice, if we found agent keeps reporting false positive, we need to revisit policy and maybe downgrade its severity and the visibility of its findings.

288
00:37:06,350 --> 00:37:07,968
Now let's look at the map again.

289
00:37:08,128 --> 00:37:09,968
We have the agent R code.

290
00:37:10,128 --> 00:37:21,252
We can put agent R code into each of the stage here so that that became a quality gatekeeper when the code goes through the development lifecycle.

291
00:37:21,870 --> 00:37:24,710
Specifically, here we're talking about four gate.

292
00:37:25,808 --> 00:37:28,848
First one is the self-review.

293
00:37:28,928 --> 00:37:35,588
The agent should always run local verification and self-review before delegating to another agent or to human.

294
00:37:36,430 --> 00:37:51,748
And second, we need independent agent reviewers, just like human diff workflow, could also check this continuous ingression signals to decide whether it needs to flag any blocking signals.

295
00:37:52,430 --> 00:37:55,204
And third, landing decision.

296
00:37:55,534 --> 00:38:06,916
Agents should be able to evaluate the risk of a change and decide which one could be merged automatically into the code base and which need to be reassigned to human for actual check.

297
00:38:07,502 --> 00:38:16,964
And fourth, we can introduce a new agent that used app end-to-end, just like human, and that would catch the bug slip through from the previous stages.

298
00:38:20,238 --> 00:38:25,968
The Git one, the claim here is your reviewer should not be a human in the first place.

299
00:38:26,208 --> 00:38:28,900
Agent should always review its own changes.

300
00:38:29,678 --> 00:38:34,288
And the benefit is like similar to local early bug fixing.

301
00:38:34,606 --> 00:38:42,724
When the agent have all the contact it's much more efficient to fix the bug rather than expecting a later stage could catch the bug and enforce another fix.

302
00:38:55,488 --> 00:39:02,288
Not because the model is not smart enough, and sometimes just because the tool it uses is not well designed for the agent.

303
00:39:02,448 --> 00:39:09,888
For example, wrong flags, inventing identifiers, error name that doesn't really tell you what the error is.

304
00:39:10,382 --> 00:39:17,248
So historically we've been designing these dev tools for human, but nowadays we need to make them friendly for the agent as well.

305
00:39:17,408 --> 00:39:19,684
So that could help improve the code quality.

306
00:39:21,966 --> 00:39:26,372
The second gate is the independent agent code reviews.

307
00:39:27,502 --> 00:39:36,772
It's quite hard to execute the system behavior dynamically to cover the changes, and that's what SAP is trying to solve.

308
00:39:38,254 --> 00:39:43,648
However, deploying a model reviewer to perform a static check is much, much easier.

309
00:39:43,918 --> 00:39:51,588
But the hard part is how can we make sure its precision is high so that developers do not tend to ignore its findings.

310
00:40:10,254 --> 00:40:10,528
And one potential practice here to improve that precision is we could codify the domain-specific conventions into the code base so that agent doesn't flag conventions as the false positive findings.

311
00:40:10,528 --> 00:40:23,364
And also to approximate precision, we monitor the developer actions and fixes on the agent reported findings so that we can feed that feedback back to the agent for future improvement.

312
00:40:25,742 --> 00:40:28,484
The key three is the landing policy.

313
00:40:28,974 --> 00:40:35,684
Not every change has the same risk level and they don't deserve the equivalent verification.

314
00:40:36,208 --> 00:40:51,428
So, for example, as shown here, there are a bunch of tiers like no op changes or refactoring usually low risk, and they may be merged into the code base automatically without actual verification.

315
00:40:52,142 --> 00:41:05,476
And if a change touched the core functionalities and could have a large blast radius, for those cases we can pair with the policy to recharge the review to a human for actual safety.

316
00:41:08,206 --> 00:41:14,372
So, this is an example where the agent could collaborate with the human policies to hold the quality bar.

317
00:41:16,846 --> 00:41:23,716
And Git 4 is about the agent that can drive the running product for end-to-end correctness backfinding.

318
00:41:24,398 --> 00:41:29,604
The agent would export app as a way the user would like dog footing.

319
00:41:29,936 --> 00:41:34,340
So where we our employees used app before our users do.

320
00:41:34,830 --> 00:41:38,052
And now we could deploy the agent to do that as well.

321
00:41:38,446 --> 00:41:49,476
It would judge what it sees without needing specification or test suite, and it could check against screenshot and the app logs to detect any errors.

322
00:41:50,030 --> 00:41:59,172
And for any findings, it could file a report just like human and include reproduction steps and evidence for developer to debug.

323
00:42:00,558 --> 00:42:10,244
If you look at this, it's actually exactly the same idea as sappings and follow the same cycle, explore, observe, judge, and report.

324
00:42:10,734 --> 00:42:19,908
And the main difference is sapping judge based on the inexplicit article of crash finding, while here we use the agent itself for checking correctness as well.

325
00:42:22,574 --> 00:42:23,936
So now the loop closed.

326
00:42:24,206 --> 00:42:28,996
Back in 2016, we introduced SIPIN at a post-land stage for crash finding.

327
00:42:29,616 --> 00:42:34,692
We move it earlier for during review time to prevent bugs.

328
00:42:35,406 --> 00:42:43,524
And now we have a new type of article with agent, which can perform correctness check, which sees the same position where SIPIN used to see it.

329
00:43:07,056 --> 00:43:12,292
I have four questions left with the room, maybe for future research.

330
00:43:12,976 --> 00:43:15,972
First is calibrating a probabilistic article.

331
00:43:16,622 --> 00:43:18,724
The agent article sometimes could be wrong.

332
00:43:19,056 --> 00:43:24,868
How can we improve that so that make it reliable that developer could trust it?

333
00:43:25,422 --> 00:43:28,676
And second, verifying the verifier.

334
00:43:30,460 --> 00:43:37,700
Agent reviewer can score diff change overall quite well but it may confidently make claims that are wrong.

335
00:43:38,094 --> 00:43:47,908
So how can we improve those cases to ensure those findings are not costing trust and hurt the developer's future trust for the findings?

336
00:43:48,430 --> 00:43:52,516
And third, we need benchmarking that do not get outdated.

337
00:43:53,198 --> 00:44:02,756
Testing verification agent and review agent, they need the benchmark to stay refreshed so that agent can build a loop to keep self-involved and improve.

338
00:44:03,662 --> 00:44:05,840
And fourth, where do human go?

339
00:44:06,766 --> 00:44:09,696
Which change do we still need human to review?

340
00:44:09,856 --> 00:44:14,016
Which change do we need human to read the code and understand?

341
00:44:14,176 --> 00:44:16,516
And how much code should the human understand?

342
00:44:17,806 --> 00:44:21,456
And the human used to play as a role as gatekeeper for quality.

343
00:44:21,646 --> 00:44:23,780
Do we need to delegate that role as well?

344
00:44:24,654 --> 00:44:27,620
And if we're successful, where do human developers need?

345
00:44:30,542 --> 00:44:33,936
Finally, let me do a summarize summary.

346
00:44:34,496 --> 00:44:42,180
In 2016, we could find one type of bug, the crash, because the crash would announce itself.

347
00:44:42,798 --> 00:44:52,484
And in the past decade, we gradually shift that test and verification earlier and extending to more types of bugs, one oracle at a time.

348
00:44:53,454 --> 00:45:00,292
And now we have a new type of Oracle, a learned oracle, based on agent, which is broad but also unreliable.

349
00:45:00,686 --> 00:45:06,212
And the interesting part is how can we let autonomous agent self-verify with that Oracle?

350
00:45:07,758 --> 00:45:12,480
So Sibbean Sibbeans was born here in Academia, here at ISTA.

351
00:45:13,326 --> 00:45:20,100
And after 10 years, we are still asking the same question: can we find the bugs that the user would find before they do?

352
00:45:21,422 --> 00:45:39,108
So finally, I'd like to sincerely thank the ISTA organizers and thank my co authors, Mark Harmon and Yue Jia, and also my colleagues at Facebook, uh, and WhatsApp who worked with me along this journey from seepings to autonomous agent.

353
00:45:40,014 --> 00:45:40,388
Thank you.

